o a  crawl demand for production has  skyrocketed and who does that remind us  of our good red enveloped friend of  course now to be clear netflix hasn't  dedicated itself to the postmodern  project the way that the cool kids at  art school have they're just trying to  get more eyes on their movies for longer  periods of time to be clear we're not  saying all movies used to be  masterpieces some cinema used to try to  be art while some was content being  popcorn blockbusters and netflix has  tried both strategies to amass its giant  portfolio of original work all in  attempts to achieve its supreme goal of  maximizing watch time it's not even up  to us to say if that came at the expense  of quality because netflix's cool ted  sorandos has implied as much himself in  response to critics panning netflix's  film bright surrando's defended the film  saying that critics speak to specific  audiences who care about quality or how  objectively good or bad a movie is not  the masses implying of course that the  masses don't care about objective  quality and that netflix is okay not  actually delivering it this reminds us  of philosopher theodore adorno's  critique of mid 20th century popular  music as scholar c.w smith explains  adorno believed that people were  listening to popular music without  actually listening because they were  experiencing what he called a breakdown  in mediating subjectivity or the  inter-subjectivity of the experience  between the subject and the piece of  music that is to say people were so  inundated with homogeneous pop music  that they started consuming it  mindlessly rather than engaging with any  single song thoughtfully for adorno this  was about more than just spacing out  though he contended that this sort of  undiscerning consumption of art  indicated a broader social tendency  towards obedience even an acceptance of  authoritarianism which can prevent us  from becoming free-thinking reflective  individuals that is to say when we stop  thinking critically about the art we  consume we become less autonomous and  instead more pliable suggestible and  easier to coerce the interminable  sameness adorno observed he believed led  to both a regression and quality of  hearing and the more or less stunting of  experience such that the listener was  docile and afraid of anything new and  that sense of safety and familiarity  sounds pretty familiar when we think  about netflix as we've discussed netflix  films are made for the sort of watching  non-watching adorno diagnosed in  mid-century music listeners and there's  reason to believe viewers might indeed  be afraid of anything new stephen  follows founder of vod click stream  which analyzes netflix data argued in a  podcast that when people watch netflix  they're sitting down and watching stuff  that is comfortable that is easy to  understand that won't challenge them the  appeal of the algorithm is its  infinitely watchable lack of  confrontation and look we're not  monsters we love us some easy watching  as much as we love frank sinatra lord  knows i've watched the episode of the  office where dwight quotes mussolini  about 76 times  blood alone moves the wheels of history  but does constant easy watching come at  the expense of our ability to really  watch and when arguably the world's most  powerful media corporation commits  itself to making movies we're all but  guaranteed to half watch does it change  the way we engage with the medium  overall and when we consider the  algorithmization of most aspects of our  modern lives the problem starts to feel  way scarier but hey at least we get to  watch is it cake but what do you guys  think when it comes to netflix films are  we actually getting the cinema we want  or merely the cinema the algorithm  thinks we want what's more  are we inadvertently creating our own  cinematic hell when we hate watch some  of netflix's more mediocre content let  us know in the comments big thanks to  our patrons for all your support please  go check out our revamped patreon page  we have lots of good stuff on there hit  that subscribe button like you're  jumping up to give the tall girl a high  five and don't forget to ring that bell  and as always thanks for watching later  [Music]  you
 

